.TH LOOK 1
.SH NAME
look \- find lines in a sorted list
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B look
[
.B \-df
] [
.BI \-t c
]
string
[ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Look
consults a sorted
.I file
and prints all lines that begin with
.IR string .
It uses binary search.
.PP
The options
.B d
and
.B f
affect comparisons as in
.IR sort (1):
.TP 4
.B d
`Dictionary' order:
only letters, digits,
tabs and blanks participate in comparisons.
.TP 4
.B f
Fold.
Upper case letters compare equal to lower case.
.TP 4
.BI \-t c
Set the termination character to
.IR c :
the comparison stops there rather than at the end of the line.
.PP
If no
.I file
is specified,
.I /usr/dict/words
is assumed with collating sequence
.BR \-df .
.SH "SORTED MEANS SORTED IN BYTE ORDER"
.I Note:
lines are compared a byte at a time, and a byte is an unsigned value from 0 to 255
on this machine where it was a signed one on the PDP\-11.
So a character above U+007F \- which is more than one byte in this system's UTF\-8 text \-
sorts
.I after
every ASCII character.
That is the order
.IR sort (1)
produces and the order
.I /usr/dict/words
below is in;
.B "LC_ALL=C sort \-c"
is the check.
A list sorted any other way will make the binary search miss entries that are there.
.PP
The two comparison options are defined over a multi\-byte character as follows,
which v7 had no occasion to say:
a byte above 0177 is part of a letter, so
.B \-d
keeps it,
and a multi\-byte letter has no case here, so
.B \-f
leaves it alone.
.B "look го"
therefore finds
.BR год ,
.B голова
and
.BR город .
.SH FILES
/usr/dict/words
.SH BUGS
.I Note:
.I /usr/dict/words
on this machine is a short list written for the purpose \- about a thousand entries \-
and not v7's 25,000-word dictionary, which is not in this source tree.
.I Look
is a working command over it, but it is not a spelling authority.
.PP
A dictionary line longer than 249 bytes is cut before it is compared.
.I Note:
v7 had no bound here at all and wrote past the end of its buffer.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sort(1), grep(1), comm(1)
